1. File and Directory Concepts

Definition:
A file stores data, while a directory (folder) stores files and other directories.

Example:
/home/user/document.txt   # File
/home/user/Documents      # Directory


2. Absolute vs Relative Path
Absolute Path
Definition: Path starting from the root directory (/).
Example:
/home/user/file.txt

Relative Path
Definition: Path relative to the current directory.
Example:
Documents/file.txt

3. Listing Files (ls)
Definition: Displays files and directories.
Example:
ls
ls -l

4. Creating Files (touch)
Definition: Creates an empty file.
Example:
touch file.txt

5. Creating Directories (mkdir)
Definition: Creates a new directory.
Example:
mkdir myfolder

6. Removing Files (rm)
Definition: Deletes files.
Example:
rm file.txt

7. Removing Directories (rmdir)
Definition: Deletes an empty directory.
Example:
rmdir myfolder


8. Copy Files and Directories (cp)
Definition: Copies files or directories.
Example:
cp file.txt backup.txt
cp -r folder1 folder2

9. Move/Rename Files (mv)
Definition: Moves or renames files/directories.
Example:
mv file.txt newfile.txt
mv file.txt /home/user/


10. View File Content (cat, less, more)
cat
Definition: Displays complete file content.
Example:
cat file.txt
less
Definition: Views file content page by page.
Example:
less file.txt
more
Definition: Displays file content one screen at a time.
Example:
more file.txt

11. File Permissions Basics
Definition: Controls who can access a file or directory.
Example:
-rw-r--r--

12. Read, Write, Execute Permissions
> Read (r)
Definition: View file contents.
Example:
r
> Write (w)
Definition: Modify file contents.
Example:
w
> Execute (x)
Definition: Run a file as a program.
Example:
x

13. Permission Representation
Symbolic Representation
Definition: Uses letters (r, w, x).

Example:
rwxr-xr--

Numeric Representation
Definition: Uses numbers.

Example:
755
644

14. Changing Permissions (chmod)
Definition: Changes file permissions.

Example:
chmod 755 script.sh
chmod u+x script.sh

15. Changing Ownership (chown)
Definition: Changes the owner of a file.
Example
chown user file.txt

16. Changing Group (chgrp)
Definition: Changes the group of a file.
Example:
chgrp developers file.txt

17. Default Permissions (umask)
Definition: Sets default permissions for new files and directories.

Example:
umask 022

18. Special Permissions
SUID (Set User ID)
Definition: Executes a file with the owner's privileges.
Example:
chmod 4755 program

SGID (Set Group ID)
Definition: Executes a file with the group's privileges.
Example:
chmod 2755 program

> Sticky Bit
Definition: Only the file owner can delete files in a shared directory.

Example:
chmod 1777 /shared

19. File Types in Linux
> Regular File (-)
Definition: Stores data.
Example:
file.txt

> Directory (d)
Definition: Contains files and folders.

Example:
Documents/

> Symbolic Link (l)
Definition: Shortcut to another file.
Example:
ln -s file.txt link.txt

> Character Device (c)
Definition: Handles character-based devices.
Example:
/dev/tty

> Block Device (b)
Definition: Handles block storage devices.
Example:
/dev/sda

> Named Pipe (p)
Definition: Used for process communication.
Example:
mkfifo mypipe

> Socket (s)
Definition: Used for network communication.
Example:
/var/run/docker.sock


20. Access Control Lists (ACL) Basics
Definition: Provides additional permissions beyond owner, group, and others.

Example:

setfacl -m u:john:rwx file.txt
getfacl file.txt